 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot I)$, where $N$ is the number of primitives and $I$ is the number of iterations (40,000). Pruning reduces $N$ over time.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$, proportional to the number of active primitives.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Evaluated on an NVIDIA A6000 GPU. Achieves high fidelity in novel view synthesis tasks [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Eliminates variable density mechanism, simplifying implementation. Frequency-modulated loss enhances high-frequency details.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of pruning thresholds and shape reset intervals. May be sensitive to initial SfM point quality.
-*   **When to Use:** When high-frequency detail is critical and computational resources allow for 40,000 iterations.
-*   **When Not to Use:** For real-time applications requiring fewer iterations or limited hardware.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The optimization runs for 40,000 iterations [S1]. The frequency-modulated loss adds computational overhead due to mask generation and filtering, but the elimination of variable density mechanisms may reduce the complexity of densification steps compared to standard 3DGS [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is determined by the number of surviving primitives after pruning. The shape parameter $\beta$ adds a small per-primitive memory overhead [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Evaluated on an A6000 GPU, GES achieves a balance between high fidelity and efficiency in novel view synthesis tasks [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Eliminates the need for a variable density mechanism; enhances high-frequency detail reconstruction via frequency-modulated loss [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of frequency masks and pruning thresholds; sensitive to high-frequency noise in the DoG filter [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The GES Optimization Strategy was introduced in the paper "GES: Generalized Exponential Splatting for Efficient Radiance Field Rendering" (CVPR 2024) [S1]. It builds upon the principles of 3D Gaussian Splatting but introduces a shape parameter $\beta$ to control the exponential decay of splats. The method was developed to improve reconstruction quality without the complexity of variable density mechanisms.
+The GES Optimization Strategy was introduced in the paper "GES: Generalized Exponential Splatting for Efficient Radiance Field Rendering" [S1]. It builds upon the principles of 3D Gaussian Splatting but introduces generalized exponential splats with a shape parameter $\beta$ to control density and shape adaptively [S1].
 
